---
// Figure: the slow-reader race with and without a lease.
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import Choice from '../shared/Choice.astro';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="inv-lease" title="A lease lets the delete cancel a fill in progress" controls="steps"
  caption="A sequence of steps, not a timed simulation. The lease is a placeholder value written with SET … NX PX 2000; the fill is SET … IFEQ, which Redis 8.4 and later provide. Each Redis command and reply shown was observed on Redis 8.10.">
  <Choice slot="params" name="lease" label="The reader" value="with"
    options={[{ value: 'without', label: 'fills without a lease' }, { value: 'with', label: 'takes a lease first' }]} />
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>Without a lease, the race ends as before: the reader's late SET stores £39 after the writer's delete, and Redis serves it for an hour.</p>
    <p>With a lease, the reader that misses first writes a placeholder, lease:r1, with NX and a 2-second expiry, and only then reads £39 from the database. When the writer commits £35 and deletes product:42, the delete removes the placeholder. The reader's fill is conditional on the key still holding lease:r1, so Redis refuses it and the old price is never stored. The next reader misses, takes a new lease, reads £35 and stores it.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { switchSequence } from './switch-sequence.mjs';
  import { leaseSpecs } from './sequences.mjs';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="inv-lease"]').forEach(root => switchSequence(root, leaseSpecs, 'lease'));
</script>
